-- title:
--   Closed points of the rigid j-chart read rational floor places
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ denotes `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`; for a valuation subring $A$ the symbol $\kappa(A)$ denotes its residue field `IsLocalRing.ResidueField A`.
--
--   **Data over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and over $\kappa(A)$.** A prime $q$ with $5 \le q$, a nonzero natural number $M'$ with $q \nmid M'$, and a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ for which $q$ is a non-unit of $A$ (this is `A.LiesOverPrime q`). A finite set $W$ of places of the field `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` over $\kappa(A)$ — that field being the subfield of `LaurentSeries (ResidueField A)` generated over $\kappa(A)$ by `jqModC` and by its $q^{M'}$-substitution `jqNModC _ M'` — together with the hypothesis `hW` saying that the members of $W$ are exactly the places lying in `ssPlaces q M' (ResidueField A)`, i.e. those $w$ satisfying `IsSupersingularPlace q M' (ResidueField A) w`; and a distinguished element $s$ of $W$. Here a place of $F$ over a base field $K$ is a valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $K$, distinct from $F$ and a principal ideal ring, with $\mathrm{ord}$ the associated normalised integer-valued order function.
--
--   The hypothesis `hle` requires `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` $\le$ `fieldBar q M'`, where the former is the subfield of `LaurentSeries ℚ̄` generated over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ by the coefficientwise images of `modularFunctionFieldFull M'` (itself the subfield of `LaurentSeries ℚ` generated over $\mathbb Q$ by the divisor expansions of level $M'$), and the latter is `xHFunctionFieldBar (q ^ 2 * M') (levelH q M')`, the level subgroup `levelH q M'` being the kernel of the reduction map $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb Z/q)^\times$, that is, the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$.
--
--   **The constant reduction.** A term $R_0$ of type `ConstantReduction A ↥(modularFunctionFieldBar M') (modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M')`: a valuation subring `R₀.integers` of `modularFunctionFieldBar M'`, a ring homomorphism `R₀.residue` from it onto the level-$M'$ field over $\kappa(A)$, and a map `R₀.placeMap` on places, subject to the axioms of that structure: an element of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lies in `R₀.integers` exactly when it lies in $A$; `R₀.residue` is surjective with kernel the maximal ideal of `R₀.integers`; it is compatible with the residue map of $A$ on constants; every nonzero element becomes, after multiplication by a suitable constant, an integral element with nonzero residue; `placeMap` preserves degrees of places; and the order function of a divisor of an integral element with nonzero residue is computed, after `Finsupp.mapDomain placeMap`, by the order function of its residue. The hypothesis `hR₀` requires, for every Laurent series $y$ with coefficients in $A$ whose coefficientwise image in `LaurentSeries ℚ̄` lies in `modularFunctionFieldBar M'`, that this image lie in `R₀.integers` and that its `R₀.residue`, read as a Laurent series over $\kappa(A)$, be the coefficientwise reduction of $y$.
--
--   **The arithmetic base.** An intermediate field $k_0$ of $\mathbb Q \subseteq \overline{\mathbb Q}$ and an element $\pi_0$ of $k_0$ whose image lies in $A$. Write $A_0 :=$ `A.comap (algebraMap ↥k₀ (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))` for the induced valuation subring of $k_0$. The hypotheses `hdvr`, `hunif`, `hhens`, `hres` require that $A_0$ be a discrete valuation ring, that its maximal ideal be generated by $\pi_0$, that $A_0$ be a henselian local ring, and that $\kappa(A_0)$ be algebraically closed; `hκ` requires that every $a \in A$ differ from some element $c$ of $k_0$ lying in $A$ by an element of the maximal ideal of $A$.
--
--   **The Tate-side function field.** A prime $\ell'$ with $\ell' \ne q$, $3 \le \ell'$ and $\ell' \nmid M'$; an element $\xi$ of $k_0$ that is a primitive $(q\ell')$-th root of unity, and `hιξ` asserting the existence of a ring homomorphism $\iota : k_0 \to \mathbb C$ with $\iota(\xi) = \exp(2\pi i/(q\ell'))$. An intermediate field $K$ of $k_0 \subseteq$ `LaurentSeries ↥k₀` which, by `hK`, equals `laurentBaseChange ↥k₀ (xHFunctionField ((q * ℓ') ^ 2 * M') (levelH (q * ℓ') M'))`, where `levelH (q * ℓ') M'` is the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/(q\ell')^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb Z/q\ell')^\times$, i.e. the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q\ell'$. Instances make $K$ an $A_0$-algebra compatibly with $k_0$. Finally an element $j$ of $K$ whose underlying Laurent series is the coefficientwise image `coeffEmb ↥k₀ jq` of the classical $q$-expansion of the modular invariant, with $j \ne 0$.
--
--   **Moduli-theoretic hypotheses.** The equivariance hypotheses `hℓ` and `hM`: for every $A_0$-algebra $T$, every Weierstrass curve $W$ over $T$ and every variable change $C$, if a quadruple $D = (x_P,y_P,x_Q,y_Q)$ is a level-$\ell'$ structure on $W$ (both points satisfy the affine equation, both $x$-coordinates are roots of `W.preΨ ℓ'`, and the two independence elements `indepElt` are units) then `D.variableChange C` is one on $C \bullet W$; and if a polynomial $h$ satisfies `IsGamma0PowAt W p k h` (namely `W.IsTwoKernel h` when $p^k = 2$, and `W.IsCyclicGenKernel p k h` otherwise) then `kernelVariableChangeDeg C (gamma0PowDeg p k) h` satisfies the same condition for $C \bullet W$, where `gamma0PowDeg p k` is $1$ if $p^k = 2$ and $\varphi(p^k)/2$ otherwise and `kernelVariableChangeDeg C d h` is $u^{-2d}\,h(u^2X + r)$ in the notation of $C$. A family of group laws $\mathcal G$ over $A_0$, assigning to each $A_0$-algebra $T$ and each projective Weierstrass curve with unit discriminant a relative group law, subject to `h𝒢` (each such group law admits a points-evaluation) and `h𝒢O` (each admits an origin chart section $\chi$ with $\chi(\mathrm{xOverY}) = \chi(\mathrm{zOverY}) = 0$). A level transport $\mathcal T$ for $\mathcal G$ and $q$, i.e. a functorial transport of raw Drinfeld pairs along $A_0$-algebra maps and along variable changes, compatible with identities and composition and preserving the level-$q$ condition, subject to `h𝒯` (`IsSectionTransport`, two clauses, summarised here: the transported sections, composed with the canonical comparison of projective models, agree with the original sections, respectively with their base changes). The hypotheses `hVC` and `hCO` provide, for each $A_0$-algebra $T$, each projective Weierstrass curve over $T$ and each variable change $C$, respectively each $A_0$-algebra map $f : T \to T'$, a graded ring homomorphism between the graded coordinate rings `projModelGradingCR` of the two projective models, under which the irrelevant ideal of the target is contained in the image of the irrelevant ideal of the source, and which is a variable-change homomorphism, respectively a coefficient homomorphism, on the classes of the constants and of the three coordinates.
--
--   **The moduli package and the chart.** A term $P_0$ of type `LevelModuliPackageAbs A₀ (rigidDataPow A₀ ℓ' M' q hℓ hM 𝒢 𝒯).toLevelModuliDatum`: an $A_0$-algebra `P₀.B₀` with a universal point, such that every point of the datum with values in an $A_0$-algebra $T$ is the image of the universal one under a unique $A_0$-algebra map, denoted `P₀.classify`. Here `rigidDataPow` is the rigidification of the product of the $\Gamma_0$-prime-power component at $M'$, the level-$\ell'$ component and the Drinfeld level-$q$ component, its points over $T$ being raw data modulo variable changes and `jOf` being the $j$-invariant. A point $x_0$ of this datum with values in $K$, with `hx₀` requiring that `jOf x₀`, read as a Laurent series over $k_0$, be `jqNModC ↥k₀ (q * ℓ')`, the $q^{q\ell'}$-substituted expansion of $j$. Writing $C :=$ `chartAlgFin A₀ ↥K j` for the subalgebra of $K$ consisting of the elements integral over $A_0[j]$, an $A_0$-algebra map `clC` from `P₀.B₀` to $C$ is given, with `hclC` requiring that the image in $K$ of `clC b` be `P₀.classify x₀ b` for every $b$. Finally a ring homomorphism $\theta : \kappa(A_0) \to \kappa(A)$ with `hθ` requiring that $\theta$ intertwine the residue maps of $A_0$ and of $A$ along the inclusion $A_0 \subseteq A$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every ideal $y$ of $C$ that is maximal and every $A_0$-algebra homomorphism $\psi : C \to \kappa(A_0)$ whose vanishing locus is exactly $y$ (that is, $\psi(c) = 0$ if and only if $c \in y$, for all $c \in C$), there exists a place $r$ of `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` over $\kappa(A)$ such that:
--
--   (i) $r$ is rational, i.e. $\kappa(A) \to \kappa(r)$ is surjective;
--
--   (ii) the element `jGeomGen (ResidueField A) M'`, namely the class of `jqModC` in the level-$M'$ field over $\kappa(A)$, lies in the valuation subring of $r$;
--
--   (iii) for every Laurent series $gf$ over $\mathbb Q$ lying in `modularFunctionFieldFull M'` such that its coefficientwise image, viewed as an element of `modularFunctionFieldBar M'`, lies in `R₀.integers`, and which satisfies the regularity condition that for every place $P$ of `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $0 \le P.\mathrm{ord}$ of the image of `jq` one also has $0 \le P.\mathrm{ord}$ of the image of $gf$, and such that moreover `qExpand ↥k₀ ℓ' (coeffEmb ↥k₀ gf)` — the $q^{\ell'}$-substitution of $gf$ over $k_0$ — lies in $K$ and, as an element of $K$, lies in the chart algebra $C$: both
--
--   (a) `R₀.residue` of the image of $gf$ lies in the valuation subring of $r$, and
--
--   (b) $\theta\bigl(\psi(\,gf(\mathsf q^{\ell'})\,)\bigr) = r.\mathrm{evalAt}\bigl(\mathtt{R_0.residue}\ gf\bigr)$, where `evalAt` denotes the value at the rational place $r$, computed by inverting $\kappa(A) \to \kappa(r)$ on the residue of the element (and $0$ if the element is not integral at $r$).
--
--   This is the Tate-side reading statement for the integral two-chart model of the level-$q\ell'$ modular curve: a closed point of the chart algebra over the maximal ideal of $A_0$ determines a single rational place of the level-$M'$ floor function field over the residue field of $A$, at which every suitably integral and cusp-regular level-$M'$ modular function is regular and takes the value prescribed by the closed point. It is the place-theoretic repackaging of [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.exists_algHom_forall_apply_residue_eq_apply_qExpand_of_eq_map_classify_rigidDataPow`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.exists_algHom_forall_apply_residue_eq_apply_qExpand_of_eq_map_classify_rigidDataPow), and it feeds the identification of the moduli point and the width/cardinality computation at points of the special fibre.
